<h2>A family shareholding and a contested transfer</h2>
<p>The plaintiffs and one of the defendants belong to a family group that holds half the shares in a private food-industries company under a Memorandum of Understanding dated 12 December 2022. When a group member proposed transferring 1,45,833 shares to a person outside the group, the plaintiffs gave conditional consent on 20 January 2026: the shares were not to travel onward to anyone outside the group without unanimous written consent. They alleged the condition was promptly breached — bids were invited, and a highest bidder was declared at Rs 216 per share on 26 June 2026. Their CS(COMM) in the Delhi High Court sought declarations of invalidity, an injunction, and specific performance.</p>

<h2>The jurisdiction objection</h2>
<p>The contesting defendant objected under Order VII Rule 10 CPC, pointing to Clauses 9(f) and 10 of the MoU, which select the courts at Bareilly — where the MoU was also signed. The single judge returned the plaint on 6 October 2026, and the Division Bench has now affirmed.</p>
<p>The appellate reasoning proceeds in familiar steps, each worth noting for drafting practice. First, an exclusive jurisdiction clause is enforceable if the chosen court otherwise possesses territorial jurisdiction — a condition satisfied at Bareilly. Second, a dispute "arising out of or relating to" the MoU falls within such a clause, and plaintiffs cannot escape it by the way the plaint is drafted or by the choice of defendants arrayed. Third, under Section 20 CPC the plaintiffs had to establish Delhi\'s jurisdiction independently, and could not: the contesting defendant did not reside in Delhi and no material part of the cause of action arose there. A co-defendant\'s Delhi residence did not displace the agreed forum. Fourth — and most pointedly — the doctrine of approbation and reprobation barred the plaintiffs from suing on the MoU\'s rights while rejecting its forum clause. Their conditional consent letter contained no jurisdiction clause of its own, and two other Delhi suits between the parties established nothing, territorial jurisdiction never having been decided in them.</p>

<h2>What was left open</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>The Bench confined itself to venue. It expressed no view on the merits of the transfer, the bidding, or the MoU\'s arbitration mechanism, and it preserved the plaintiffs\' remedies at Bareilly — including the shelter of Section 14 of the Limitation Act, which can exclude time spent prosecuting proceedings in good faith before a court lacking jurisdiction. A returned plaint, in other words, is a detour rather than a defeat, provided limitation is managed with care.</p>
</div>

<h2>Drafting and strategy lessons</h2>
<p>Three lessons follow. For transactional drafters: a forum clause in a family or shareholder MoU will be read as meaning what it says, so choose the forum deliberately at signing, not by litigation afterthought. For plaintiffs: before filing a commercial suit in Delhi on a contract with a foreign forum clause, test the Section 20 CPC foundations independently — residence of the contesting defendant and the situs of the material cause of action — because artful pleading will not supply what the statute requires. For defendants: an Order VII Rule 10 objection taken early, before the merits are joined, remains the cleanest exit from an inconvenient forum, and this judgment confirms that the Division Bench will protect that exit on appeal.</p>
